                           Whats new in PCBoard
                           ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


  NOTE:  PCBoard v15.21 primarily exists to introduce FIDO which did not make
  it into the v15.2 release.

  The following is a BRIEF summary of the FIDO features available in this
  release:

     Built in mailer
     ---------------
     A. Seamless FIDO login. FIDO negotiation invisible to human callers.
     B. Supports multiple networks.
     C. Definable events for dial out control.
     D. Supports EMSI, FTS-0006 and FTS-0001 protocols.
     E. Manual or automatic execution.
     F. Nodelist compiler and node-diff processing.
     G. File Request and File send capabilities.
     H. Event driven and forced polling.
     I. Session, Packet and areafix password protection.
     J. Inbound and outbound routing.

     Built in mail tosser
     --------------------
     A. Supports FIDO and non-FIDO specific echos.
     B. Netmail. (seamless internet style addressing)
     C. *.MSG (.TIC processing available through third party)
     D. Secure conference access.
     E. Command line execution.
     F. Fast import speed.

  You can read more about FIDO in the FIDO.DOC file.  However, in addition to
  FIDO, the following other enhancments have also been made since the v15.2
  release:


  PCBOARD.EXE
  -----------
  - Added a SET PCB=/NORIP switch to avoid automatic RIP detection completely.
    This can speed up the initial login sequence of connect.

  - Added a SET PCB=/PAD switch which forces PCBoard into PAD mode (Packet
    Switch Network) without setting it in PCBOARD.DAT.

  - Added SET PCB=/NOVIEWSEC setting which can be used to ignore the FSEC
    "security level" requirement when using the F;V command to view the
    contents of files.

 - Added SET PCB=/NOPROTECT setting which is used to *disable* PCBoard's
   protection of alias names.  At this point in time, it is used to disable:

     a) The automatic increase in required security for the USERS command
        inside of a conference where aliases are allowed.
     b) The ignoring of "real names" by the soundex search when performed in
        a conference where aliases are allowed.

 - SHARED SECRET PASSWORD LOGINS

   One problem with the internet is just how "open" it really is.  A hacker
   equipped with a TCP/IP packet "sniffer" could capture logins to a system
   looking for password information inside the packets, then later use that
   information to log into the system.

   Admittedly, it would take a very serious hacker to go to that kind of
   effort, but it *is* possible for it to happen.

   To help combat the situation, and make your PCBoard logins more secure,
   PCBoard (/M only) now supports the MD5 Shared Secret algorythm.  This
   allows a caller to log into the system using a code which cannot be
   decrypted, but can only be used to confirm a "shared secret", in this case,
   his password.

   What happens is this:  PCBoard sends a unique identifier at login, a string
   which PCBoard will never repeat again, and the caller's system captures
   that string and uses it as a key in encrypting the caller's password.  The
   caller's system then sends back a 128-bit number.  This is not a copy of
   the password (encrypted or otherwise), but rather, it is like a digital
   "fingerprint" which can be used to securely establish a match.  PCBoard
   then compares that 128-bit number to it's own 128-bit number, generated
   from the original unique string it sent out combined with what it knows the
   caller's password to be.

   If both PCBoard and the caller generate the same 128-bit number, then the
   passwords match and the caller is allowed to log in.

   What does the caller see?

       At login, PCBoard sends the unique string, but backspaces it out.
       Chances are, the caller won't even see it or have to worry about it.
       The caller's system automatically captures the string, encodes his
       password, and sends back the 128-bit number.  All of this occurs behind
       the scenes and the caller sees nothing different.

       At the point where the caller is to log in, he types in his first and
       last name, after pressing enter, PCBoard logs him into the system
       *without* asking him for his password since the password can already be
       identified based on the 128-bit number it received.  (unless the number
       doesn't match, in which case PCBoard prompts the caller for a password)

    How is this done?

       To be done effictively, the caller's system has to be able to receive
       the unique string, perform the MD5 calculations, and send the 128-bit
       number back all on its own.  The best place for this to be implemented
       is in the comm program (such as Telix, Procomm, etc) as part of a login
       script, for example.

    But how can I use it today?

       Ray Gwinn has implemented the MD5 algorythm into VMODEM.  Effectively,
       *every* comm program that dials out through VMODEM can inherit this
       capability without any changes whatsoever.  To use it, you need to have
       SIO version 1.42 or later.  Then, using VMODEM, you would telnet into
       the BBS by giving the ATDT command, followed by the name or IP address,
       followed by the password to be used.

       For example, you could reach Salt Air like this:

               ATDT 199.67.41.2 "MYPASSWORD"

       VMODEM will take "MYPASSWORD" and perform the MD5 algorythm on it as
       soon as it receives the unique string from the BBS, then it will send
       back the 128-bit number.  Again, all of this occurs seamlessly - the
       end-user will see nothing different, except that the BBS will not
       require him to enter a password at login.

   If I would like to read more about this algorythm, or if I am developing a
   comm program, where can I get more information about this MD5 algorythm for
   implementation into my own software?

       Read RFC documents 1321 and 1725.  You can obtain copies of these files
       on the internet at FTP.PSI.COM.  Or you can get information and source
       code from Ray Gwinn by sending a message to ray@gwinn.com asking for a
       copy of MD5.ZIP.

  - Added a new @-macro modifier (similar to 'R' and 'C').  The new one is 'T'
    for TRUNCATE.  This one will truncate the length of an @-macro to fit the
    field length defined.  For example, if you use the following:

         @user@
         @user:10@
         @user:10T@

    The first display will be left-justied displaying *only* the length of the
    user name.  The second one will also be left justified and if the user name
    is 10 characters or less, it will fill a full 10 characters.  If the name
    is LONGER than 10 characters, it will show the complete name without any
    truncation.  The third one is also left justified, if the user name is less
    than 10 characters, it will be padded to 10.  But if the user name is
    longer than 10 characters, it will be TRUNCATED down to just 10 characters.

    Another idea for usage:  if you have long conference names, you might need
    this in order to make the conference names fit on your screen.

  - Improved FOSSIL efficiency (more so in ZMODEM than PCBoard).

  - Changed the Bulletin Search capability so that it will now look for records
    within the bulletin based on lines which start with either a dash (-) or
    equals sign (=) in the first column.  PCBoard will then display only the
    "records" that match rather than displaying the entire bulletin.

    If separators ARE being used in the bulletins, then the top portion of the
    bulletin (the header) will be displayed prior to displaying any search
    matches - but only if a match is found.  In other words, if you type "B A S
    FINDTHIS" and the word FINDTHIS is found in bulletins 3 and 5, then the
    header for 3 will be displayed, followed by the records in bulletin 3 that
    match it, then the header for bulletin 5 and the records in bulletin 5 that
    match.

  - Made a modification so that if you have accounting enabled and you are
    charging callers for time spent online, then if a file is marked as NOTIME
    (or FREETIME) then after downloading the file PCBoard will record a
    "FILETIME CREDIT" equal to the time spent downloading the file.

  - Modified the recycle process so that if a Packet Switch Network environment
    is in use PCBoard will avoid leaving DTR up at drop to DOS.

  - Modified the "Slow Drive" support to pull files off of slow drives in
    sorted drive order in order to reduce CD swapping on a CD-ROM changer.

 - Modified the FORWARD command so that it will not allow you to forward a
   generic message (such as @user@ or @##@) unless your security level is high
   enough to copy or move messages (normally a sysop-only function).

 - Removed the 2K requirement for COMM-DRV buffer size.  You can manually edit
   the COMM-DRV configuration file and reduce the buffer sizes.  Then, upon
   loading COMTSR it will use less memory.

 - Modified the routines that take the modem off hook so that, if the system
   is configured for a PAD environment (packet switch network), where taking
   the modem off hook is not possible, PCBoard will turn DTR off to indicate
   that it is not ready for calls.  Also, in a few other areas where PCBoard
   may need to do some processing before recycling due to carrier loss, it
   will drop DTR to try to prevent any further calls from getting thru the PAD
   until it is ready to handle them.  This modification was also applied to
   the Zmodem modules to handle carrier loss during file transfers as well.

 - PCBoard now monitors the CONNECT string to determine if a TELNET connection
   via VMODEM is being established.  VMODEM indicates a TELNET connection by
   appending "/TEL" to the CONNECT string.  If a TELNET connection is
   established, it will be noted and recorded in the pcboard.sys file.  This
   information is then utilized by Zmodem to adjust the method by which
   control characters are transferred.

 - Modified the caller-id reporting in the caller log so that if the string is
   too long to fit on one line in the log it will spill over to the next line.

  PPL Processing
  --------------
  - Added findfirst and findnext functions.

    Syntax:
      findfirst(filespec)
      findnext()

    EXAMPLE 1:
    STRING str
      println "Directory listing of *.*"

      str = findfirst("*.*")
      println str
      while(str != "") do
        str = findnext()
        println str
      endwhile
      println "Done!"

    EXAMPLE 2:
    STRING str
      println "Deleting *.*"
      DELETE findfirst("*.*")
      while(str != "") do
        str = findnext()
        DELETE str
      enswhile
      println "Done!"

    Both functions only return the filename.  Any other information normally
    returned in a findfirst/findnext implementation, such as file size, data
    time attributes, can be obtained through the fileinf function.  This way
    the string will not need to be parsed in PPL and execution will be much
    faster.

 - Added a new statement to the language:

     COMMAND ViaCmdLst,CommandLine

       ViaCmdLst
         A boolean value indicating whether or not to try to find the command
         in CMD.LST (TRUE means search CMD.LST, FALSE means do not; if TRUE
         and the command is not in CMD.LST, it will try the standard commands
         automatically, failing if the command does not exist)

       CommandLine
         A string value with the command and parameters to execute
         (like "R A Y O S")

   What does this mean?  It means, simply, that you can have a PPE do some
   setup processing, then run the message editor like this:

     COMMAND FALSE,"E"

   Then *regain* control at the statement *after* COMMAND and continue!  No
   more messy state files and keyboard stuffing just to run other commands!
   That's not to say that keyboard stuffing doesn't have it's place (it will
   continue to be very useful), but for certain types of processing, COMMAND
   can't be beat.  An example PPS file:

     CLS
     PRINTLN "You are about to enter a message..."
     WAIT
     COMMAND FALSE,"E SCOTT ROBISON"
     CLS
     PRINTLN "You are about to read a message..."
     WAIT
     COMMAND FALSE,"R O L"
     CLS

   As you can see, this allows you flexibility that wasn't possible before
   now.

   NOTES!!!  Not all portions of PCBoard are re-entrant.  For example, you
   should not try to have two message editor processes active at the same time
   (in other words, you shouldn't launch the message editor from within a MNU
   and then launch a PPE from a shifted function key that tries to enter
   another message).  So you'll need to be careful about *nested* COMMAND (or
   equivalent) calls.  But sequential processing shouldn't be a problem at
   all.

   If it is determined at some point in the future that allowing this
   flexibility causes more problems than it solves, the COMMAND statement will
   be scaled back to ensure that attempts are not made to re-enter code.  So
   use it well and wisely!

 - Due to a possible overflow in DATE values, the valid range of dates has
   been changed from 1/1/1900-12/31/79 to 1/1/1900-12/31/78.

   Dates from mm/dd/79 to mm/dd/99 will be considered 1900's, while dates from
   mm/dd/00 to mm/dd/00 to mm/dd/78 will be considered 2000's.

   NOTE:  Processing has also been changed to allow you to convert dates from
   the string form "mm/dd/yyyy" as well as the "mm/dd/yy" format.  In other
   words, you can now set DATE = "01/01/1936" and it will properly convert the
   date value.  However, conversion back (date back to a string) will still
   result in a 2-digit year (i.e. if STRING="01/01/1936" and you used the
   commands DATE=STRING then STRING=DATE, the final value of STRING would be
   "01/01/36").


  PCBSETUP.EXE
  ------------
  - Added an "Ignore Empty Security Level" setting on the accounting
    configuration screen to accomodate setups where the users should remain at
    the same security level all the time and only grant or deny access to
    system functions based on the account balance.

  - Added an ALT-V command to the Event Editor which lets you view a different
    node's last date run values without having to exit the Event Editor screen.

 - Modified the DECOMP.BAT field on the UUCP screen so that you can press the
   F2 function key to edit the file.

 - Modified the event editor to remove the restriction that prevents event
   times from 23:59 to 00:01 from being entered.  Valid range is now 00:00 to
   23:59.


  PCBFILER.EXE
  ------------
  - Added a /NODOS command line parameter which disables the ability to shell
    out to DOS.

 - With PCBFiler's "Scan for Duplicate or Missing Files" feature, PCBFiler can
   quickly scan all of the drives on the system to determine what files are
   duplicate or missing and make use if the IDX files to speed the process up
   even further.

   However, if the IDX is pointing to a slow CD-ROM (worse yet, is if the
   CD-ROM is a CD changer where the CD may not even be in the drive yet) it
   can take awhile to check the file size and date since that information is
   not stored in the IDX file and requires that PCBFiler call DOS's findfirst
   function to locate the file on the slow drive.

   Keying off of the "Slow Drive" list (found in PCBSetup), PCBFiler will now
   check the slow drive list to see if the file is on a slow drive before
   reporting on it.  If the file is found on a slow drive, then it will avoid
   checking for the date or size of the file and will instead just show the
   file name and path from where it was found together with a message
   indicating that the file is on a slow drive.

   If the date and size are necessary to the report you are generating, then
   it is recommended that you temporarily empty the Slow Drive list in
   PCBSetup (i.e.  blank it out).


  PCBSM.EXE
  ---------
  - Added the following command line switch:

        PCBSM /EDIT:first;last

    This will jump to the specified user in the user record.  Note the colon
    separator between keyword and name, and the semi-colon separator between
    the first and last names.  (i.e. put semi-colons where spaces would
    normally go in the user name)


  PCBPACK.EXE
  -----------
  - Added a /IGNORE command line parameter which will ignore PACKOUT dates in
    messages.

 - Added a new /COMPRESS command line parameter.

   The /COMPRESS switch is a little dangerous in that it does NOT create any
   temporary or backup files.  Instead, it packs the file "in place".  In
   other words, it takes your MSGS file and compresses the contents of the
   file, removing packed-out messages simply by moving the next active message
   that comes after the packed-out message into the space previously held by
   the packed-out message.

   This new option brings with it the following advantages:

   1) Assuming most of the messages near the beginning of the file will not
      have to be packed out, the packing process will go faster since PCBPack
      can simply skip over messages which are not being deleted rather than
      having to "copy" those messages into a temporary file.

      However, if you are packing by date, chances are good that the messages
      near the front of the file will need to be packed out which will cancel
      this advantage since PCBPack must then copy the message within the same
      file to move it to its new location.

      So /COMPRESS improves speed the greatest on message bases which do not
      need to be packed at all, or on message bases in which the messages that
      are being removed are nearer to the end rather than the beginning of the
      message base.

   2) Time spent deleting the old backups, and renaming the original files to
      the backup names, and renaming the new files to the original filenames,
      is completely avoided and could result in a small time savings.

   3) Packing "in place" means that PCBPack does NOT require any additional
      disk space, because no new files will be created.  So the check for free
      space is avoided which can reduce the packing time as well.

   4) And because the packing is performed "in place", without the use of
      temporary files, it means that there will be a lot less disk
      fragmentation that is caused by running PCBPack.  (however, remember
      that the normal every day operation of adding messages into the message
      base will still fragment the files and using /COMPRESS will actually
      avoid *defragmenting* the message base, so don't stop defragmenting your
      hard disk just because you're using the /COMPRESS switch).

   Notes on the /COMPRESS switch:

   - The /KILLBAK switch is unnecessary when using /COMPRESS since no backup
     files are made.

   - The ESC key is *disabled* in the middle of the conference, because when
     you are compressing the file, you cannot abort in the middle without
     messing up the file.  So if you press ESC, PCBPack will wait until it
     finishes the conference it is working on before aborting the pack.

   - A reminder:  Because no backups are made, rebooting the machine in the
     middle of a pack will potentially cause problems.  It would be best if
     the machine performing the pack were run on a UPS (uninterruptable power
     supply) in order to avoid such problems.  The problems themselves won't
     be too serious ...  re-running PCBPack will fix any actual problems ...
     but you may end up with duplicate messages in the message base so the
     recommended solution would be to run PCBPack with the /KILLDUPS option.

 - In addition to the /COMPRESS switch, there were also a couple of other
   speed optimizations applied to PCBPack which affect the regular packing
   methods as well (not just the /COMPRESS switch).

 - Some fido mail tossers are putting messages into the PCBoard message base
   with invalid dates in them.  The only evidence you usually see of this is
   that PCBPack then complains saying that it has encountered an invalid
   header in the message base.  Effectively, PCBPack ends up removing the
   messages which have the invalid dates in them.

   To help you to see the problem, I have added a /NODATE command line
   parameter to PCBPack which disables the "date validation" routine that is
   used as part of the header verification before deciding whether or not a
   message header is valid.  To use it, you would type something like this:

          PCBPACK /AREA:100 /NODATE

   By using the /NODATE option, PCBPack will avoid packing out messages that
   would have been removed due to having a bad date.

   !!!WARNING!!!  In so doing, that means you will end up with whatever date
   on the message that the tosser put onto it.  The intention here is to give
   you some way of providing feedback to the author of the tosser so that he
   can fix his software.  But the drawback is that if you pack out by DAYS
   only then PCBPack may not delete the message for a long time (if the
   invalid date is a futuristic date) or it may delete it immediately (if the
   invalid date is outside of the number days that you keep mail).  It is
   therefore suggested that you at least add a /MAX:  switch to the command
   line to keep the number of messages down to a reasonable maximum so that
   even messages with futuristic dates will eventually be packed out.

 - Modified the /KILLALL switch so that, if used by itself, PCBPack will scan
   thru the messages and remove any file attachments encountered along the
   way.  You can speed up the /KILLALL switch by using /FAST.  When /FAST is
   used, PCBPack simply truncates the message base.  This is very fast, but
   if there were any file attachments, they are then orphaned and will have
   to be removed manually.


  ZMSEND.EXE
  ----------
  - Made changes which help ZMSEND to work better on an internet connection by
    dynamically adjusting timeouts.

 - Added a /TELNET command line option.  When used, Zmodem avoids sending
   control characters which helps increase the likelyhood that Zmodem will
   work for a particular TELNET setup (though some setups will still have
   problems).  This option is also in ZMRECV.


  ZMRECV.EXE
  ----------
 - Added support for receiving 8K packets (for FIDO)


 MKPCBMNU.EXE
 ------------
 - Added two new option types to the option editor:

     CLST
       Attempt to process the specified parameters as a command line to
       PCBoard via the CMD.LST file (if the command is not defined in CMD.LST
       it will try the standard commands automatically, failing if the command
       does not exist)

     CMD
       Attempt to process the specified parameters as a command line to
       PCBoard without searching the CMD.LST file

   What does this mean?  It means, simply, that you can have a MNU run a
   command without having to give up control via keyboard stuffing!  For
   example, up to now you'd need a command like this to run the message
   editor:

        Keyword    Sec   Type   Parameters
        컴컴컴�    컴�   컴컴   컴컴컴컴컴
     1) E          0     STFQ   E @PARAMS@

   Now all you need is:

        Keyword    Sec   Type   Parameters
        컴컴컴�    컴�   컴컴   컴컴컴컴컴
     1) E          0     CMD    E @PARAMS@

   This allows you to run commands without ever having to leave the MNU
   environment!  That's not to say that keyboard stuffing doesn't have it's
   place (it will continue to be very useful), but for certain types of
   processing, CLST and CMD can't be beat.  As you can see, this allows you
   flexibility that wasn't possible before now.

   NOTE:  Not all portions of PCBoard are re-entrant.  For example, you should
   not try to have two message editor processes active at the same time (in
   other words, you shouldn't launch the message editor from within a MNU and
   then launch a PPE from a shifted function key that tries to enter another
   message).  So you'll need to be careful about *nested* CLST/CMD (or
   equivalent) calls.  But sequential processing shouldn't be a problem at
   all.


 PCBDIAG.EXE
 -----------
 - Made changes to bring PCBDIAG up to date with v15.21.  (NOTE:  only the
   main fido screen is printed out, the rest of the fido information, which is
   stored in PCBFIDO.CFG, is not printed out)


  What's New in PCBoard/UUCP
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  UUUTIL.EXE
  ----------
  - Added support for importing a FIDONET.NA file (/MSGS & /FIMPORT switches).

      Example command line.

      UUUTIL /START:<NUMBER> /TEMPLATE:<NUMBER> /MSGS:<PATH>
        /FIMPORT:C:\FIDONET.NA

      /START    is the number to start adding conferences.
      /TEMPLATE is the conference number to use for defaults.
      /MSGS     is a path to store the message files in *MUST* include the
                trailing backslash.
      /FIMPORT  has the path to a FIDONET.NA style file.

      Fidonet.NA is formatted as follows.

      <Tagname> <Description>

      The tagname is the Fidonet area name.
      The Description is used for the name of the PCBOARD conference.

   Also added the following switches for FIDO processing:

     /USEAREA   - Will use the area tag as the conference name instead of the
                  description.

     /FSETNAMES - Will set all information for FIDO conferences except for the
                  message base name.


  UUOUT.EXE
  ---------
  - Added support to scan a "References:" line from the body of a message.

  - Modified log file processing to allow reading the file whether or not it
    was being updated.

  - Added start date and time to program logging.

  - Added support to help off-line readers that don't add distribution lines.

  - Modified screen display code to get more info in less space.

 - Added -f switch to force UUOUT to perform alias translation for short from
   names as well as long from names (the long from name is the user@domain
   internet style address; the short from name is the PCBoard user name in
   parenthesis after the user@domain).


  UUXFER.EXE
  ----------
  - Added multi-tasker friendly code to give up time not being used.

  - Modified log file processing to allow reading the file whether or not it
    was being updated.

  - Added start date and time to program logging.

  - Modified screen display code to get more info in less space.

 - Added support for file grading.  Add -gX (where X is the grade to use) to
   your UUXFER command line.  Grading *can* allow your site to request only
   certain types of files (such as email only instead of email and news)
   from your service provider.  For specific information on how grading
   might help your feed, contact your service provider about their
   support of file grading.

 - Added support to automatically detect BUSY reports from the modem and
   redial a configurable number of times.  The -b command line switch is used
   to specify how many attempts to make.  The way it works is simple:  If the
   expect sequence is CONNECT (no more, no less), BUSY will also be watched
   for.  If BUSY is found, it will issue the previous send sequence (which
   should be the dial command for your service provider).  For example:

      UUXFER -s uucpnet -t 60 -b 10

   The above command line would redial up to ten times.


  UUIN.EXE
  --------
  - Added code to rename data files (*.BAG/*.D) to *.BAD (and bad execute
    files from *.X to *.BAX) on certain error conditions involving bad packets
    and to simply keep them on errors saving the messages.

  - Added some conditional code to help reduce the size of the UUIN.LOG file.
    The default (debug level 0) is to keep the same amount of logged
    information.  If you use the -x switch to specify the debug level, the
    following values are valid at this time:

       0    = All debug information
       1- 9 = Type of packet/filename/spool/error logging
      10-19 = Decompressing/*.X/*.BAG logging
      20-?? = Individual message logging

  - Added support for tracking multi-part encoded files and automatically
    decoding them.  This process involves tracking messages in temporary files
    for up to a week in order to gather all parts of a multi-part post
    together.  Most things that *can* be imported immediately *is* imported
    immediately instead of going to temporary files.  If the -u switch is used
    to disable automatic UUDECODING, no temporary files are tracked.  If
    temporary files processed, they will be stored as random file names of the
    form XXXXXXXX.XXX.  The X's are random hexa-decimal digits (0-9 and A-F).
    A file named MSGINFO.DAT will be tracked with or without the -u switch.  It
    is a simple database of information about the temporary files (0 bytes if
    there are no temporary files).

    NOTE:  Due to this new processing, the speed of UUIN has dropped noticably.
    I believe it is still faster than most programs, but the demand for this
    feature has mandated it's inclusion at the expense of some processing
    speed.  Hopefully it won't put that big a drain on most systems.

  - Modified log file processing to allow reading the file whether or not it
    was being updated.

  - Added start date and time to program logging.

  - Added support for PageSat mail bags.

  - Modified screen display code to get more info in less space.

 - Added -o switch to output a list of newsgroups not found on your system
   during import.  The file will be named JNKGROUP.LST and stored in your base
   uucp directory.  It will be a simple text file, one newsgroup per line,
   with duplicates.  For example, if you have 4 messages come in for
   alt.bbs.bob, and you don't have alt.bbs.bob, you will have 4 lines with
   alt.bbs.bob.  You will need to sort the file and remove duplicates before
   creating the new conferences.

 - Added support for an addition Date: header line format.

 - Added code to copy text from the original message when bouncing.
   NOTE:  This will only copy the text you would have imported into your
   message base after stripping any undesired headers, so you may wish to keep
   selected extra headers (such as TO:  FROM:  DATE:  SUBJECT:) so that the
   original sender will get sufficient information to know who/why their
   message bounced.

 - Added -d switch to force UUIN to use the current system date and time when
   posting messages instead of the date and time from the Date: header in the
   message header.

   This can be useful for showing when a message was imported, while keeping
   the internet date header inside the message body to indicate when the
   message was written.  It also avoids packing out a message too soon when
   the message is delayed several days before being received and imported.

 - Added -g switch to force UUIN to giveup time slices during message imports
   and exports.  This switch is not necessary for UUIN operation, though you
   may wish to use it if you are doing continuous processing for a satellite
   connection and don't want UUIN to be as greedy with system resources.



  PCBEDIT.EXE
  -----------
  - Added one macro (@real@) that was left out from v1.10 (the PCBoard
    v15.1 update) and six new macros that were added to PCBoard v15.2
    (@credleft@, @crednow@, @credstart@, @credused@, @xoff@, @xon@)

  - Added support for the new T (Truncate) format justification specifier.


  PCBMAIL.EXE
  ~~~~~~~~~~~
  Message Editor
  ==============
  -   Ctrl-left/Ctrl-right for next previous word
  -   Ctrl keys for CUT/COPY/PASTE/CLEAR to clipboard
  -   Holding Shift key to mark text
  -   If you change the login configuration dialog box location of the
      PCBOARD.DAT filename PCBMail will now tell you that you need to exit
      PCBMail for changes to take place. After dialog box is displayed
      PCBMail will then terminate.
  -   Display only destination filename when downloading a file.
  -   Delete key deletes selected block in message editor

  General
  =======
  -   Added prompting user when new message is detected that has been
      written to them.
  -   Jumping to messages dialog box will default to the referenced
      message.
  -   Display only destination filename when downloading a file.
  -   Changed conference selection dialog box to page in and out
      conference names, so that all of the conference name could be
      viewed.
  -   UUOUT wasn't exporting messages written in PCBMail
      because PCBMail wasn't saving messages with ECHO flags set.
  -   Added Hot keys to the following commands
      - Conference Selection          ALT S
      - Jumping to messages           ALT J
      - Check for new mail            ALT K
      - Mailing list dialog box       ALT M
      - Message Filter dialog box     ALT T
  -   Password protection when selecting conferences.
  -   Displays forwarding extended header information in message body.
 -   Added feature that allows the user to skip over the login dialog box
     and directly log the user into the system.  If the information
     contained in the startup name/password is incorrect then PCBMail
     will tell the user that the name/password that was entered was
     incorrect and then it brings up the login dialog box.  This option
     is available through the startup configuration options.
 -   Optimized the read message window so that PCBmail will display
     messages much quicker.  Especially when blocking text and display is
     much quicker when viewing large messages.

 -   Optimized the message editor.

 -   Sped up the message quoting feature.



***  Don't forget to read FIDO.DOC to find out more about FIDO. ***
